Package: lenovo-yoga-c940-14iil-support
Architecture: all
Depends: convertible-helper (>= 1.3.0), convertible-power (>= 1.0.0), grub-large-console (>= 1.0.0),
         firmware-iwlwifi, firmware-intel-misc, firmware-intel-graphics, firmware-sof-signed,
         alsa-ucm-conf, alsa-topology-conf, iio-sensor-proxy, bluez, udev, ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: thermald, bolt, v4l-utils, fprintd, libpam-fprintd, intel-media-va-driver
Description: Lenovo Yoga C940-14IIL (81Q9) hardware support for Debian
 Settings and firmware dependencies for the Lenovo Yoga C940-14IIL (type 81Q9,
 Ice Lake Core i7) on a stock Debian GNOME desktop: Wi-Fi and Bluetooth
 firmware (AX201), Smart Sound (SOF) audio firmware and UCM profiles, Intel
 graphics firmware, auto-rotate (iio-sensor-proxy), tablet mode
 (convertible-helper), power management (convertible-power plus this laptop's
 TLP settings) and readable GRUB text (grub-large-console).
 .
 The fingerprint reader (06cb:00be) has no free driver and cannot be made to
 work from the Debian archive; fprintd is only recommended.  See README.md.
 .
 The settings are applied only when the DMI product version is
 "Lenovo Yoga C940-14IIL".
Package: lenovo-yoga-c940-14iil-windows-vm
Architecture: all
Depends: qemu-system-x86, qemu-system-modules-spice, qemu-utils, libvirt-daemon-system, libvirt-clients,
         virt-manager, virt-viewer, ovmf, swtpm, swtpm-tools, passt, virtiofsd, gdisk, dosfstools, mtools,
         dmsetup, libhivex-bin, util-linux, procps, ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: lenovo-yoga-c940-14iil-support
Description: run the existing Windows install of a Lenovo Yoga C940 in a KVM virtual machine
 Boots the Windows installation already on the laptop's NVMe drive in a libvirt/QEMU
 virtual machine (UEFI, virtual TPM 2.0, the firmware's OEM key passed through so Windows
 stays activated, SPICE display with clipboard and USB redirection, ssh forwarded to host
 port 2222), using a small virtual GPT disk with its own EFI system partition and the real
 Windows partition mapped in with device-mapper.  A libvirt hook unmounts /windows before
 the VM starts and remounts it afterwards, and refuses to do either while it would be unsafe.
 .
 The default mode is an overlay: the real partition is never written.  Writing to it
 (so the same Windows state is shared with a native boot) is a separate, explicit switch.
 See the README.
